# Hoppscotch Community Edition 2026.7.0 environment schema (@env-spec / varlock format).
#
# The compose pins hoppscotch/hoppscotch:2026.7.0, the all-in-one (AIO)
# Community Edition image: one container running Caddy (app on :3000, admin
# dashboard on :3100, backend reverse-proxied on :3170), the NestJS backend
# (listening on :8080 inside), and the Go bundle server for the desktop app
# (:3200).
#
# Source: grep of `process.env.*` and NestJS `configService.get(...)` across
# packages/hoppscotch-backend, packages/hoppscotch-selfhost-web (including its
# Go `webapp-server`), packages/hoppscotch-common and packages/hoppscotch-sh-admin
# at tag 2026.7.0, cross-checked against .env.example, prod.Dockerfile's `aio`
# stage, aio_run.mjs and the "Install and build Community Edition" docs page.
#
# WHAT CHANGED SINCE THE GUIDES YOU MAY REMEMBER: JWT_SECRET, SESSION_SECRET,
# MAGIC_LINK_TOKEN_VALIDITY, REDIRECT_URL, the MAILER_* SMTP block and the
# GOOGLE_/GITHUB_/MICROSOFT_ OAuth block are NO LONGER environment variables.
# At this version they are rows in the `infra_config` Postgres table, seeded on
# first boot and edited from the admin dashboard's onboarding wizard. The
# backend reads them through the `INFRA.` namespace
# (`configService.get('INFRA.JWT_SECRET')`, auth/auth.module.ts,
# auth/strategies/*.ts), never from the process environment. JWT_SECRET and
# SESSION_SECRET are generated as `randomBytes(32).toString('hex')` at first
# boot (infra-config/helper.ts). You cannot and should not pass them here, and
# setting them as env vars does nothing. PROXY_APP_URL is the single exception:
# it is the only entry in SYNC_ONLY_VARIABLES, so it keeps syncing from the
# environment into that table on every boot.
#
# That makes DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEY the root of the whole secret chain; see its
# note below. DATABASE_URL and DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEY are not optional: the
# backend exits without either, and aio_run.mjs kills the container when the
# backend exits.
#
# The template bundles its own postgres:17-alpine and runs the image in SUBPATH
# mode, so DATABASE_URL, WHITELISTED_ORIGINS and the whole VITE_* block are
# PLATFORM-OWNED refs the compose fills in rather than values an operator
# types. Those are frozen against the compose character for character; the only
# two values the install prompts for are DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEY and
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD.

# @defaultSensitive=false @defaultRequired=infer
# @docs("Install and build Community Edition", https://docs.hoppscotch.io/documentation/self-host/community-edition/install-and-build)
# ---

# ─── Backend: database and encryption ────────────────────────────────────

# Postgres connection string for Prisma. There is no default and no embedded
# database: the backend's PrismaService reads this directly
# (prisma/prisma.service.ts) and the AIO container runs
# `prisma migrate deploy` against it on boot. Without it the backend process
# exits, and aio_run.mjs kills the whole container when the backend exits, so
# the service restart-loops rather than degrading. `postgresql://` and
# `postgres://` are both accepted.
#
# PLATFORM-OWNED: assembled by the compose from the prompted POSTGRES_PASSWORD
# and the bundled db service's hostname. Written as a literal it would freeze
# at install and point a second copy of this stack at the first copy's
# database; as a ref it re-resolves every deploy. Change it only to move to an
# external Postgres, which leaves the bundled one running and unused.
# @required @sensitive
# @type=string(startsWith=postgres)
# @docs("Community Edition prerequisites", https://docs.hoppscotch.io/documentation/self-host/community-edition/prerequisites)
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://hoppscotch:${POSTGRES_PASSWORD}@${{stack.db.HOST}}:5432/hoppscotch

# The AES-256-CBC key (src/utils.ts, `ENCRYPTION_ALGORITHM = 'aes-256-cbc'`,
# `Buffer.from(key)`) that encrypts every secret Hoppscotch stores in Postgres:
# the auto-generated JWT_SECRET and SESSION_SECRET that sign access/refresh
# tokens and OAuth state, the SMTP password, and each SSO provider's client
# secret. It must be EXACTLY 32 characters (31 or 33 throws at boot).
#
# Whoever holds this key can decrypt those rows out of a database dump and mint
# valid Hoppscotch sessions for any user, including admins, with no password.
# Generate it (`openssl rand -hex 16`) and treat it as the instance's master
# key. Never use the value upstream ships in `.env.example`
# (`data encryption key with 32 char`) or the asterisk placeholder printed in
# the install docs: both are public, and either one makes every stored secret
# on this instance readable by anyone who has read the same page.
#
# Changing it later does NOT re-encrypt the existing rows: the backend fails
# with ERR_OSSL_BAD_DECRYPT on the next boot and stops.
# @required @sensitive
# @type=string(matches=/^.{32}$/)
DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEY=

# ─── Backend: HTTP surface ───────────────────────────────────────────────

# Comma-separated origin allowlist for CORS, enforced whenever PRODUCTION is
# "true" (src/main.ts calls `.split(',')` on it unconditionally, so leaving it
# unset crashes the backend on boot). It must list this deployment's own app,
# admin and backend URLs; the `app://` entries are what lets the Hoppscotch
# desktop app talk to a self-hosted instance, and dropping them silently breaks
# only that client.
#
# PLATFORM-OWNED. Upstream ships three http://localhost:* entries here because
# the default multiport layout gives the app, the admin dashboard and the
# backend three different origins. This stack runs in subpath mode
# (ENABLE_SUBPATH_BASED_ACCESS below), where all three are served from the same
# host under `/`, `/admin` and `/backend`, so the deployment's public URL is
# the whole browser-facing allowlist, and it re-resolves when the domain
# changes instead of freezing at install.
# @required
# @docs("Connect the desktop app to a self-hosted instance", https://docs.hoppscotch.io/documentation/clients/desktop/setup)
WHITELISTED_ORIGINS=${{stack.hoppscotch.PUBLIC_URL}},app://localhost_3200,app://hoppscotch

# Port the NestJS backend listens on INSIDE the container. The image bakes
# PORT=8080 (prod.Dockerfile, `aio` stage) and the bundled Caddyfile
# reverse-proxies :3170 to localhost:8080, so changing this without editing the
# in-image Caddyfile makes the backend unreachable. Left at the image default.
# @type=port
PORT=8080

# The image bakes PRODUCTION="true". It is what turns on the WHITELISTED_ORIGINS
# CORS allowlist and hides the GraphQL playground (src/main.ts, app.module.ts).
# Setting it to "false" on a reachable instance opens CORS to every origin and
# exposes the playground. Do not.
# @type=boolean
PRODUCTION=true

# When "true", the backend trusts the left-most entry of X-Forwarded-For as the
# client IP (`app.set('trust proxy', true)`). Turn it on only behind a reverse
# proxy you control that overwrites that header; behind nothing, any client can
# forge its own IP for rate limiting and logs.
# @type=boolean
TRUST_PROXY=false

# Default proxy URL offered to clients when the in-app proxy toggle is on. This
# is the ONE variable still synced from the environment into the `infra_config`
# table on every boot (SYNC_ONLY_VARIABLES in infra-config/helper.ts), so a
# value set here overrides whatever an admin last chose in the dashboard.
# Requests routed through it leave your network, so point it at your own
# Proxyscotch instance, or clear the variable to manage it from the admin
# dashboard alone.
# @type=url
# @docs("Interceptor", https://docs.hoppscotch.io/documentation/features/interceptor)
PROXY_APP_URL=https://proxy.hoppscotch.io

# Read once at startup (src/main.ts): when set to anything truthy the process
# emits the GraphQL schema file and never calls `bootstrap()`, so the backend
# never listens and the container exits. It is a build-time tool, not a runtime
# switch. Leave it unset.
GENERATE_GQL_SCHEMA=

# Only consulted when an SSO login runs without a stored SESSION_SECRET: with
# NODE_ENV=production the backend throws instead of falling back to an
# ephemeral in-process secret that would break OAuth callbacks across restarts
# (auth/stateless-state-store.ts). The AIO image does not set it.
# @type=enum(production, development)
NODE_ENV=

# ─── Frontend (VITE_*, injected at container start) ──────────────────────
# aio_run.mjs collects every VITE_-prefixed variable and rewrites them into the
# already-built static bundle with `import-meta-env` before Caddy starts, so
# these take effect on restart without a rebuild. They are baked into JavaScript
# served to browsers: nothing secret belongs in a VITE_ variable.

# The URL this deployment's app is reached at, the value the app uses for its
# own links and redirects. Also decides ALLOW_SECURE_COOKIES: the backend sets
# secure cookies only when this starts with `https`
# (infra-config/helper.ts, `determineAllowSecureCookies`), so an https
# deployment described here as http hands out session cookies without the
# Secure flag.
# PLATFORM-OWNED, and https, so the backend hands out Secure session cookies.
# @required @type=url
VITE_BASE_URL=${{stack.hoppscotch.PUBLIC_URL}}

# Base URL used when generating shareable short links. Usually the same as
# VITE_BASE_URL. PLATFORM-OWNED.
# @required @type=url
VITE_SHORTCODE_BASE_URL=${{stack.hoppscotch.PUBLIC_URL}}

# URL of the admin dashboard. On the AIO image in its default multiport mode
# that is this host on port 3100; in the subpath mode this stack runs it is
# `/admin` on the one public URL. PLATFORM-OWNED.
# @required @type=url
# @docs("Community Edition admin dashboard", https://docs.hoppscotch.io/documentation/self-host/community-edition/admin-dashboard)
VITE_ADMIN_URL=${{stack.hoppscotch.PUBLIC_URL}}/admin

# GraphQL endpoint the app calls, `<backend>/graphql`. The backend is published
# on port 3170 by the AIO image in multiport mode, and under `/backend` in the
# subpath mode this stack runs. PLATFORM-OWNED.
# @required @type=url
VITE_BACKEND_GQL_URL=${{stack.hoppscotch.PUBLIC_URL}}/backend/graphql

# WebSocket endpoint for GraphQL subscriptions, `<backend>/graphql` over
# ws:// or wss://. Use wss:// wherever VITE_BASE_URL is https, or browsers
# block the connection as mixed content.
#
# PLATFORM-OWNED, and the one value built from DOMAIN rather than PUBLIC_URL:
# PUBLIC_URL already carries the `https://` scheme and this needs `wss://`, so
# the bare domain is the only piece that can be reused. Every deployment here
# is https, so wss is always right.
# @required
# @type=string(startsWith=ws)
VITE_BACKEND_WS_URL=wss://${{stack.hoppscotch.DOMAIN}}/backend/graphql

# REST endpoint the app and admin dashboard call, `<backend>/v1`. This is the
# most-read variable in the frontend; a wrong value looks like an app that
# loads and then cannot sign anyone in. PLATFORM-OWNED.
# @required @type=url
VITE_BACKEND_API_URL=${{stack.hoppscotch.PUBLIC_URL}}/backend/v1

# Optional links shown in the app's footer/settings. They default to
# Hoppscotch's own hosted documents; point them at your own if you are
# operating this instance for other people.
# @type=url
VITE_APP_TOS_LINK=https://docs.hoppscotch.io/support/terms
# @type=url
VITE_APP_PRIVACY_POLICY_LINK=https://docs.hoppscotch.io/support/privacy

# Access token sent to the proxy at PROXY_APP_URL. Hoppscotch's public proxy
# needs none, so this is empty by default; a self-hosted Proxyscotch started
# with a token requires the matching value here. It ends up in the browser
# bundle, so it is shared by everyone who can load the app. It authorises use
# of your proxy, nothing more, but treat it as published.
# @sensitive
VITE_PROXYSCOTCH_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# ─── Container runtime ───────────────────────────────────────────────────

# "true" serves the app, admin dashboard and backend from ONE port under
# /, /admin and /backend (aio-subpath-access.Caddyfile) instead of the default
# three-port layout. This is also what the desktop app expects from a
# self-hosted instance. Switching it changes which ports need publishing, so
# the compose's exposed ports have to change with it.
#
# This stack turns it ON, because the edge routes ONE port per service: in the
# default layout the browser would load the app on 3000 and reach neither the
# admin dashboard on 3100 nor the backend on 3170. The compose therefore
# publishes 80 (Caddy's default, see HOPP_ALTERNATE_PORT) and every VITE_*
# value above is a path under the one public URL. Turning it back off means
# republishing 3000/3100/3170 and rewriting those URLs to match.
# @type=boolean
# @docs("Deploy and upgrade Community Edition", https://docs.hoppscotch.io/documentation/self-host/community-edition/deploy-and-upgrade)
ENABLE_SUBPATH_BASED_ACCESS=true

# Caddy's in-container HTTP port, default 80, and honoured ONLY in subpath mode
# (in multiport mode Caddy uses the fixed 3000/3100/3170). Set a port above
# 1024 to run the container under a non-root UID, and update the published
# mapping to match. Avoid 8080, 3200, 3000, 3100 and 3170, which are already
# taken inside the container. HOPP_AIO_ALTERNATE_PORT is the pre-2026 spelling,
# still honoured as a fallback when HOPP_ALTERNATE_PORT is unset.
# @type=port
HOPP_ALTERNATE_PORT=
# @type=port
HOPP_AIO_ALTERNATE_PORT=

# ─── Bundle server (desktop app support, port 3200) ──────────────────────
# The Go `webapp-server` inside the AIO image signs the web bundles the
# Hoppscotch desktop app downloads from this instance.

# Port the bundle server binds inside the container. The image publishes 3200
# and the desktop app expects it there.
# @type=port
# @docs("Connect the desktop app to a self-hosted instance", https://docs.hoppscotch.io/documentation/clients/desktop/setup)
WEBAPP_SERVER_PORT=3200

# The ed25519 private key that signs bundles, base64-encoded (64 decoded bytes
# for the full key, 32 for the seed). Checked in order: KEY, then SEED, then
# SECRET, which is any string the server derives a key from. All three are
# optional: with none set the server loads /data/webapp-server/signing.key, and
# generates and persists one if that is missing too.
#
# Anyone holding this key can sign a bundle that desktop clients of this
# instance will accept and execute. Supply one only if you need the identity to
# survive a volume wipe or to be identical across replicas; otherwise let the
# container generate it and keep the file. A short or guessable
# WEBAPP_SERVER_SIGNING_SECRET is worse than no value at all, because it is
# deterministic.
# @sensitive
WEBAPP_SERVER_SIGNING_KEY=
# @sensitive
WEBAPP_SERVER_SIGNING_SEED=
# @sensitive
WEBAPP_SERVER_SIGNING_SECRET=

# Path the signing key is read from and written to. Defaults to
# /data/webapp-server/signing.key, a directory the image pre-creates
# group-writable so the key survives restarts under a non-root UID. Without a
# volume there, the key is regenerated on every start and previously signed
# bundles stop verifying.
WEBAPP_SERVER_SIGNING_KEY_FILE=

# Go duration strings ("15s", "2m"); "0" disables the timeout. An invalid value
# logs a warning and falls back to the default rather than failing.
WEBAPP_SERVER_READ_TIMEOUT=15s
WEBAPP_SERVER_WRITE_TIMEOUT=15s
WEBAPP_SERVER_IDLE_TIMEOUT=60s

# Directory of built frontend assets the bundle server serves. The image ships
# them at /site/selfhost-web; only change this if you are mounting your own
# build over it.
FRONTEND_PATH=/site/selfhost-web

# "development" makes the bundle server look for assets at ../dist and keep its
# signing key in .webapp-server instead of /data. Not for a deployed instance.
# @type=enum(development, production)
GO_ENV=

# ─── Bundled Postgres ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Config for the stack's own postgres:17-alpine, not Hoppscotch's own settings,
# but env all the same. The AIO container runs `prisma migrate deploy` against
# it on every boot, so there is no separate migration step and no first-run
# ordering to get right beyond `depends_on`.

# Role the backend connects as, created by the Postgres image on first boot. It
# is baked into DATABASE_URL above; changing it here alone only breaks the pair.
POSTGRES_USER=hoppscotch

# Password for that role, used by both the database container and DATABASE_URL.
# The wizard generates it at install. Only ever reachable on the stack's private
# network, but it is still a full read/write path to every collection, team and
# encrypted secret on the instance, including the rows DATA_ENCRYPTION_KEY
# protects, which is the pair an attacker needs to mint sessions.
# @required @sensitive
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# Database created on first boot. Baked into DATABASE_URL above.
POSTGRES_DB=hoppscotch
